## Summary
The Société de transport de Trois-Rivières (STTR) addressed the challenge of fragmented onboard technologies in its bus fleet by repurposing an Ericsson 5G router into a unified onboard computer. This case study illustrates how integrating various systems like fare collection, displays, and cameras into a single platform can simplify maintenance and improve efficiency. The approach leverages 5G technology for enhanced connectivity and operations in public transportation.

## Key Points
- Public buses typically accumulate disparate technologies over time, leading to fragmented systems with separate hardware and maintenance requirements.
- STTR implemented an Ericsson 5G router to serve as a centralized onboard computer for its bus fleet.
- This integration consolidated services such as fare systems, passenger displays, cameras, vehicle-location tools, and communication tools into one platform.
- The solution reduces operational complexity and costs by minimizing the need for multiple hardware systems and connections.
- The case study demonstrates the practical application of 5G technology in modernizing public transportation infrastructure.


